      "writtenOn": "2025-03-17T23:36:14Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "There are multiple ways to manage the target destinations of the copy rule, and `Dsts` is one of the two ways to do so in the current implementation of prebuilt etc gen. Another way is utilizing `Relative_install_path` property. Utilizing this property allows simpler generated module definition, as seen below:\n```\n// With srcs \u0026\u0026 dsts\nprebuilt_etc {\n    name: \"some_module\",\n    srcs: [\n    \t\"file1.txt\",\n    \t\"file2.txt\",\n    ],\n    dsts: [\n    \t\"foo/bar/file1.txt\",\n    \t\"foo/bar/file2.txt\",\n    ],\n}\n\n// With relative_install_path\nprebuilt_etc {\n    name: \"some_module\",\n    srcs: [\n    \t\"file1.txt\",\n    \t\"file2.txt\",\n    ],\n    relative_install_path: \"foo/bar\",\n}\n```\n\nUtilization of `relative_install_path` property is the reason why `allCopyFileNamesUnchaged` logic exists. That being said, I assume the issue is arising when the generated `prebuilt_root` module type (which is selected as suitable prebuilt_* module type was not found) is trying to set `relative_install_path` property. Would you be able to verify if https://r.android.com/3548660 resolves the issue that you are trying to solve? I still believe that the usage of `prebuilt_any` should be limited as a fallback behavior, not the default behavior.",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 9,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 10,
        "endChar": 37
      },
      "revId": "af3bdffd6393a0ff372bca2c45f03d1f382b07f0",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
